  "answer": "**Verdict**\nThe FlameMargin prediction engine does not predict flame spread rates because there are no spread-rate measurements in the current NASA extraction. However, evaluating flammability at 18% $\\text{O}_2$ and 14.7 psia shows that SIBAL fabric sustains a flame (burns) across ventilation flows of 4 to 53 cm/s, and extinguishes at lower flows of 2 to 3 cm/s.\n\n**Key numbers**\n- Pressure: 14.7 psia\n- Oxygen concentration: 18% $\\text{O}_2$\n- Flammable flow range: 4 cm/s to 53 cm/s\n- Non-flammable flow range: 2 cm/s to 3 cm/s\n- $\\text{O}_2$ limit range: 16.4% to 17.3% depending on flow velocity\n\n**Evidence**\nSupported by NASA BASS-II test data on SIBAL cotton-fiberglass fabric (NASA/TM-20210011385, Table 7.1), specifically nearest experiments GMT100-T13, GMT175-T18, and GMT45-T4.\n\n**Operational recommendation**\nThis is decision support, not a replacement for NASA-STD-6001 material testing. Do not rely on low ventilation flows (2–3 cm/s) to prevent a burn, as edges of the modeled range have the least data and higher flows readily sustain combustion. Instead, limit exposed material, isolate ignition sources, and maintain fire suppression readiness.\n\n**Limits**\nNo flame spread rate can be provided. Results apply specifically to SIBAL cotton-fiberglass fabric in microgravity at 14.7 psia.",
